"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"**Note:** I can't see the bank's iShares example, so the US points below describe typical practice and should be checked against that fund's prospectus and SAI.\n\n**Risks and controls**\n- **Borrower default:** The borrower fails to return securities. Controls include counterparty credit limits, diversifying borrowers, and agent indemnification where the contract provides it.\n- **Collateral shortfall:** After a default, liquidated collateral may not cover the cost of buying back the securities, due to market gaps or wrong-way risk. Controls include over-collateralisation, daily mark-to-market, margin calls, haircuts and concentration limits.\n- **Cash reinvestment:** Reinvested cash can lose value or become illiquid, and this loss is usually borne by the fund. Controls include conservative money-market vehicles and limits on maturity and credit.\n\n**US (1940 Act):** Collateral is typically at least 102% (domestic) or 105% (international), and lending is capped at one-third of assets.\n\n**UCITS (ESMA/2014/937):**\n- Securities must be recallable at any time.\n- Collateral must be liquid, high-quality, diversified (about 20% per issuer), valued daily and haircut.\n- Non-cash collateral cannot be reused.\n- Cash may be reinvested only in deposits, high-quality government bonds, reverse repos or short-term money-market funds.\n\nCollateral mitigates losses but does not eliminate them.","ttft_ms":4539,"type":"result","duration_ms":8798,"uuid":"4836f6f1-f2d6-4de5-ba08-89f54c99a5de","ttft_stream_ms":1063,"time_to_request_ms":72,"first_content_frame_ms":1063,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
